# Target C-II — terminal FREE transport/phase separation

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** proof-only endpoint refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** terminal full-core Type-II FREE survivor sequences with `pi D-q=o(D)` and all-phase surplus `mu_D -> mu_*`  
**Depends on:** terminal FREE surplus-endpoint stability; audited outer-geodesic direction-measure theorem; audited atomic stopped-train phase decomposition; exact stopped-train endpoint multigraph; exact arbitrary O--O multishear  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_free_terminal_transport_phase_separation_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The exact surplus endpoint has a much more rigid endpoint-transport structure than the
previous fan alternatives show. Almost every outer-face train occurrence belongs to
an outer-to-outer train, while almost every bounded exceptional-face occurrence belongs
to a bounded-to-bounded train. The bounded-to-bounded population is in turn almost
entirely concentrated in one atomic `pi/3` phase orbit.

The key point is to use the atomlessness of the geodesic outer-walk direction measure
before spending the bounded endpoint capacity. Atomic train mass cannot consume a
linear number of outer endpoints. Since the quadratic strict-rhombus crossing density
requires at least `2 sqrt(Delta_2)` atomic train mass, the bounded-to-outer transport
population is at most `2g D`, where `g=0.007707` is the matched all-train gap.

Put

\[
\Delta_2=\frac{\pi}{2\sqrt3},
\qquad
s_2=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
g=0.007707,
\]

\[
A_0:=2s_2+g
=1.912332613727915\ldots,
\]

and

\[
M_*:=4s_2+2g=2A_0
=3.824665227455829\ldots.
\]

At the exact surplus endpoint,

\[
\frac bD\to\pi,
\qquad
\frac{Q+4f}{D}\to M_*.
\tag{TP.1}
\]

Let

- `E_BB` be the number of stopped trains with both terminal occurrences on bounded
  nonquadrilateral faces;
- `E_BO` the number with one bounded and one outer terminal occurrence; and
- `L_O` the number with both terminal occurrences on the actual outer face.

Then

\[
\boxed{
\limsup\frac{E_{BO}}D\le 2g=0.015414,
}
\tag{TP.2}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{E_{BB}}D\ge 2s_2
=1.904625613727915\ldots,
}
\tag{TP.3}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{L_O}D
\ge\frac{\pi}{2}-g
=1.563089326794897\ldots.
}
\tag{TP.4}
\]

Thus the earlier `0.105849...D` O--O branch is not merely one possible endpoint
alternative: at exact surplus saturation there are at least `1.563089...D-o(D)`
O--O trains.

There is also a sharp one-parameter phase frontier. Along any subsequence on which

\[
e:=\lim \frac{E_{BO}}D
\]

exists, necessarily `0<=e<=2g`, and if

\[
B(e):=\frac{M_*-e}{2},
\]

then one exact atomic `pi/3` orbit has train mass at least

\[
\boxed{
w(e):=
\frac{B(e)+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-B(e)^2}}{2}.
}
\tag{TP.5}
\]

All bounded-to-bounded trains outside that dominant orbit have total mass at most

\[
\boxed{
r(e):=
B(e)-w(e)
=
\frac{B(e)-\sqrt{8\Delta_2-B(e)^2}}{2}.
}
\tag{TP.6}
\]

The worst endpoint is `e=0`, where

\[
w(0)=1.904609957194518\ldots,
\qquad
r(0)=0.007722656533397\ldots.
\tag{TP.7}
\]

At the opposite endpoint `e=2g`,

\[
B(2g)=w(2g)=2s_2,
\qquad
r(2g)=0.
\tag{TP.8}
\]

So throughout the entire exact-surplus transport segment, at least
`1.904609957...D-o(D)` bounded-to-bounded trains lie in one three-axis atomic
orbit, and at most `0.007722657...D+o(D)` bounded-to-bounded trains lie outside it.

The outer-to-outer population has the opposite behavior. If `lambda` denotes
Lebesgue measure on the projective direction circle `R/pi Z`, and `omega` is any weak
limit of the O--O train direction counting measures normalized by `D`, then

\[
\boxed{
omega=\frac{\lambda-\beta}{2}
}
\tag{TP.9}
\]

for some positive measure `beta<=lambda` of total mass at most `2g`. Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\left\|\omega-\frac{\lambda}{2}\right\|_{\rm TV}\le g.
}
\tag{TP.10}
\]

In particular every fixed projective interval `I` satisfies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf\frac{N_{OO}(I)}D
\ge
\max\left\{0,\frac{|I|-2g}{2}\right\}.
}
\tag{TP.11}
\]

Every fixed `pi/6` direction sector therefore contains at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(\frac{\pi}{12}-g-o(1)\right)D
=
(0.254092387799149\ldots-o(1))D
}
\tag{TP.12}
\]

O--O trains, and every fixed `pi/12` sector contains at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(\frac{\pi}{24}-g-o(1)\right)D
=
(0.123192693899575\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{TP.13}
\]

Finally, the O--O train crossing graph is a triangle-free circle graph. By the
classical theorem of Kostochka that every triangle-free circle graph is 5-colourable,
there is a pairwise noncrossing O--O family of size at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(\frac{\pi}{10}-\frac g5-o(1)\right)D
=
(0.312617865358979\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{TP.14}
\]

The audited exact O--O multishear therefore supplies at least that many simultaneous
contact-preserving shear coordinates whose split supports are laminar/dual-tree
intervals. Inside every fixed `pi/6` direction sector there is such a noncrossing
family of size at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(\frac{\pi}{60}-\frac g5-o(1)\right)D
=
(0.050818477559830\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{TP.15}
\]

This changes the live endpoint problem. The exact-surplus endpoint is not a generic
mixture of singleton, outer-loop, and flower transport. It is an almost separated
two-phase network:

```text
bounded--bounded:
  >= 1.9046256137 D total,
  >= 1.9046099572 D in one atomic pi/3 orbit,
  <= 0.0077226566 D outside that orbit;

bounded--outer:
  <= 0.015414 D;

outer--outer:
  >= 1.5630893268 D,
  direction measure within TV 0.007707 of half-Lebesgue,
  >= 0.3126178654 D in one pairwise-noncrossing exact-shear family.
```

No diameter-decreasing motion is claimed. The remaining problem is to make the
diffuse laminar O--O shear reservoir interact with the nearly saturated atomic
bounded--bounded rhombus grid and the active diameter rows.

---

## 1. Exact endpoint transport identities

The stopped-train endpoint multigraph gives

\[
2E_{BB}+E_{BO}=Q+4f,
\qquad
E_{BO}+2L_O=b.
\tag{1.1}
\]

At the exact surplus endpoint, (TP.1) therefore gives, after passing to any subsequence
on which `E_BO/D -> e`,

\[
\frac{E_{BB}}D\to B(e):=\frac{M_*-e}{2},
\tag{1.2}
\]

and

\[
\frac{L_O}D\to\frac{\pi-e}{2}.
\tag{1.3}
\]

The task is to bound `e`.

---

## 2. Atomic train mass must be bounded--bounded

Let `mu_D` be the stopped-train projective direction counting measure normalized by
`D`, and pass to the phase subsequence used by the audited atomic decomposition. Let

\[
A=\sum_j M_j
\tag{2.1}
\]

be the total atomic mass in absolute `D` normalization, where `M_j` is the mass in one
exact projective `pi/3` orbit.

The terminal outer walk is asymptotically geodesic around the near disk. The audited
outer-direction theorem therefore gives

\[
\nu_D^{out}
:=
\frac1D\sum_{\text{outer edge occurrences }a}\delta_{\phi_a}
\Longrightarrow
\lambda,
\tag{2.2}
\]

where `lambda` is Lebesgue measure on `R/pi Z`.

In particular `lambda` has no atoms. Fix the countable atomic support of the train
phase limit. For each finite collection of its atoms, choose shrinking disjoint
neighborhoods. Equation (2.2) says that the number of outer occurrences in those
neighborhoods is `o(D)` after diagonalization. Every train having an outer endpoint
uses at least one such occurrence. Hence the entire atomic train mass is carried by
bounded-to-bounded trains in the limit:

\[
\boxed{
A\le B(e).
}
\tag{2.3}
\]

This is stronger than the previous endpoint-capacity estimate `A<=A_0`, because it
subtracts the actual B--O transport before paying the atomic mass.

---

## 3. Quadratic crossing mass bounds B--O transport by `2g D`

The audited atomic decomposition gives orbit crossing masses `X_j` with

\[
X_j\le\frac{M_j^2}{4}
\tag{3.1}
\]

and

\[
\sum_jX_j=\Delta_2.
\tag{3.2}
\]

Therefore

\[
\Delta_2
\le
\frac14\sum_jM_j^2
\le
\frac14\left(\sum_jM_j\right)^2
=
\frac{A^2}{4}.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Thus

\[
\boxed{
A\ge2\sqrt{\Delta_2}=2s_2.
}
\tag{3.4}
\]

Combine (2.3), (1.2), and (3.4):

\[
2s_2
\le
\frac{M_*-e}{2}.
\]

Since `M_*=4s_2+2g`,

\[
\boxed{
e\le2g.
}
\tag{3.5}
\]

Because the initial subsequence was arbitrary, this proves (TP.2). Equations
(1.2)--(1.3) immediately give (TP.3)--(TP.4).

The constant `2g` is sharp for this scalar relaxation: at `e=2g`, bounded-to-bounded
capacity is exactly `2s_2`, and one atomic orbit of that mass can saturate the weighted
Mantel bound `Delta_2=A^2/4`.

---

## 4. Sharp dominant-orbit frontier

Fix a subsequential value `e in [0,2g]` and abbreviate

\[
B=B(e)=\frac{M_*-e}{2}.
\]

Let

\[
W:=\max_jM_j.
\tag{4.1}
\]

From (3.2),

\[
4\Delta_2\le\sum_jM_j^2.
\tag{4.2}
\]

The elementary bound `sum M_j^2 <= W A <= WB` first gives

\[
W\ge\frac{4\Delta_2}{B}>\frac B2
\tag{4.3}
\]

throughout `2s_2<=B<=A_0`. Since `W>B/2`, all residual atomic mass has total at most
`B-W<W`. Concentrating that residual in one orbit maximizes its square sum, so

\[
\sum_jM_j^2
\le
W^2+(A-W)^2
\le
W^2+(B-W)^2.
\tag{4.4}
\]

Combining (4.2)--(4.4),

\[
W^2+(B-W)^2\ge4\Delta_2.
\tag{4.5}
\]

On `[B/2,B]` the left side is increasing in `W`, so solving the quadratic gives

\[
W\ge
\frac{B+\sqrt{8\Delta_2-B^2}}2
=w(e).
\tag{4.6}
\]

As in Section 2, atomic trains in this dominant orbit have only `o(D)` outer
occurrences. Hence `(W-o(1))D` of them are bounded-to-bounded. Since the entire
bounded-to-bounded population is `(B+o(1))D`, all B--B trains outside the dominant
orbit number at most

\[
(B-W+o(1))D
\le
(r(e)+o(1))D.
\tag{4.7}
\]

This proves (TP.5)--(TP.8).

The same calculation localizes the quadratic strict-rhombus mass. All non-dominant
atomic orbits together have mass at most `r(e)`, hence crossing mass at most

\[
\frac{r(e)^2}{4}.
\tag{4.8}
\]

Crossings involving the nonatomic phase part are `o(D^2)` in the audited atomic
decomposition. Therefore the dominant orbit alone carries at least

\[
\boxed{
\left(
\Delta_2-\frac{r(e)^2}{4}-o(1)
\right)D^2
}
\tag{4.9}
\]

strict rhombi.

At the worst endpoint `e=0`,

\[
\Delta_2-\frac{r(0)^2}{4}
=
0.906884772261126\ldots.
\tag{4.10}
\]

Thus essentially the full quadratic rhombus population lies in the dominant bounded
three-axis orbit, while the O--O population below remains diffuse.

---

## 5. The O--O direction measure is almost half-Lebesgue

Define the normalized projective direction measures

\[
\beta_D
:=
\frac1D\sum_{\tau\in BO}\delta_{\phi_\tau},
\qquad
\omega_D
:=
\frac1D\sum_{\tau\in OO}\delta_{\phi_\tau}.
\tag{5.1}
\]

Every B--O train contributes one outer terminal occurrence and every O--O train
contributes two. This includes an O--O singleton: its physical bridge edge has two
outer-face occurrences. Therefore the occurrence-counted identity is exact:

\[
\boxed{
\nu_D^{out}=\beta_D+2\omega_D.
}
\tag{5.2}
\]

Pass to a weakly convergent subsequence. By (2.2),

\[
\nu_D^{out}\Longrightarrow\lambda.
\]

Because `beta_D` is literally a submeasure of `nu_D^{out}`, every weak limit `beta`
satisfies

\[
0\le\beta\le\lambda.
\tag{5.3}
\]

Its total mass is the subsequential value `e`, so Section 3 gives

\[
\beta(\mathbb R/\pi\mathbb Z)=e\le2g.
\tag{5.4}
\]

Equation (5.2) then yields

\[
\omega=\frac{\lambda-\beta}{2},
\]

which is (TP.9). Since `beta` is positive,

\[
\left\|\omega-\frac{\lambda}{2}\right\|_{\rm TV}
=
\frac{\|\beta\|_{\rm TV}}2
=
\frac e2
\le g.
\tag{5.5}
\]

For every fixed projective interval `I`, its endpoints have zero `beta` mass because
`beta<=lambda`, so weak convergence gives

\[
\omega(I)
=
\frac{|I|-\beta(I)}2
\ge
\frac{|I|-e}{2}
\ge
\frac{|I|-2g}{2}.
\tag{5.6}
\]

This proves (TP.10)--(TP.13).

The conclusion is stronger than saying there are many O--O trains. A linear O--O
population is forced in every fixed direction sector, and its limiting direction
measure can differ from half-Lebesgue by total variation at most `0.007707`.

---

## 6. O--O trains form a triangle-free circle graph

The following topological observation turns the diffuse O--O population into a large
laminar shear reservoir.

### Lemma 6.1 — O--O crossing graph is a circle graph

Ignore O--O singleton trains for a moment. Replace the embedded contact graph by a
small regular neighborhood, resolve repeated outer-face vertices into their physical
outer-walk occurrences, and cap every inner boundary component. The result is a
topological disk. Every non-singleton O--O train midpoint path becomes a proper simple
arc in this disk with two distinct endpoints on its boundary. Capping the inner
components does not alter any train path or any train-path intersection.

By the audited train pseudosegment theorem, two distinct train arcs meet transversely
at most once. Two proper arcs in a disk have odd mod-two intersection number exactly
when their four boundary endpoints alternate. Since the actual intersection count is
zero or one, two train arcs cross if and only if their endpoint pairs alternate.

Replacing every train arc by the straight chord joining the corresponding two points
on an abstract circle therefore preserves the intersection graph. Hence the
non-singleton O--O train crossing graph is a circle graph.

An O--O singleton contains no strict rhombus and is an isolated vertex of the train
crossing graph. Adding isolated vertices preserves the circle-graph conclusion.
`square`

The full train crossing graph is simple and triangle-free by the audited finite train
theorem. Its O--O induced subgraph is therefore a triangle-free circle graph.

A theorem of Kostochka (1988) says every triangle-free circle graph is 5-colourable.
A convenient later source stating this result is A. A. Ageev, *Every circle graph of
girth at least 5 is 3-colourable*, Discrete Mathematics 195 (1999), 229--233,
doi:10.1016/S0012-365X(98)00192-7. Ageev's abstract records the Kostochka
5-colour theorem and the sharp 5-chromatic triangle-free examples.

Therefore every O--O train set contains an independent set of at least one fifth of
its vertices. In train geometry, an independent set is a pairwise noncrossing train
family. Applying (TP.4) proves (TP.14). Applying the same theorem to the induced
subgraph in any fixed `pi/6` sector and using (TP.12) proves (TP.15).

The constant five is best possible for arbitrary triangle-free circle graphs, so any
improvement of the factor `1/5` must use additional train geometry.

---

## 7. Exact multishear consequence

The audited exact arbitrary-O--O multishear applies to every finite O--O family. Thus
(TP.4) already gives

\[
\left(\frac{\pi}{2}-g-o(1)\right)D
\]

simultaneous exact contact-preserving shear coordinates on the full source.

For the pairwise noncrossing family from Section 6, the cut system is laminar after
rooting the complement dual tree. Every source pair is separated by exactly the
train cuts on one dual-tree path, so its signed split support is an interval in that
tree order. Hence (TP.14) gives an exact laminar multishear of dimension at least

\[
(0.312617865358979\ldots-o(1))D.
\tag{7.1}
\]

For every fixed `pi/6` direction sector, (TP.15) gives a laminar exact multishear with

\[
(0.050818477559830\ldots-o(1))D
\tag{7.2}
\]

coordinates whose train directions all lie in that sector.

No claim is made that these motions preserve the diameter bound. Section 152's
two-sided diameter bracing applies to every individual O--O coordinate, and the exact
multishear formulas describe all active-diameter first and second variations. The new
gain is that this system is now simultaneously large, nearly direction-uniform, and
laminar on a linear subfamily.

---

## 8. Remaining obstruction

At exact terminal FREE surplus saturation, the prior fan split has collapsed into a
more rigid transport decomposition. Up to the small `g`-budget:

1. bounded-to-bounded trains form one near-Mantel atomic `pi/3` orbit carrying almost
   every strict-rhombus crossing;
2. outer-to-outer trains use almost the entire outer boundary occurrence budget and
   have an almost half-Lebesgue direction measure;
3. bounded-to-outer trains are limited to at most `0.015414D+o(D)`;
4. a `0.312617865...D` O--O subfamily is pairwise noncrossing and supports an exact
   laminar multishear.

This does not yet contradict C-II. The next useful theorem should couple the
near-saturated atomic B--B grid to the diffuse laminar O--O shears. Two concrete
routes remain:

- prove that a near-disk source cannot route `Theta(D)` direction-diffuse O--O trains
  through or around a B--B train system carrying
  `(0.906884772...-o(1))D^2` rhombi while keeping the O--O rhombus incidence
  `o(D^2)`; or
- use the laminar signed-split matrix of the `0.312617865...D` exact O--O shear family,
  together with two-sided active-diameter bracing, to produce a simultaneous
  diameter-nonincreasing non-Euclidean motion.

No theorem-state file is changed.
